{"title":"Archimedean property","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The <b>Archimedean property</b> is a fundamental property of numbers stating that there are no \"infinitely large\" or \"infinitely small\" elements. Named after the ancient Greek mathematician <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Archimedes\" title=\"Archimedes\">Archimedes</a> of Syracuse, it is the property that allows us to show that the set of integers (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/0/d4023589a374e5b4a1bc6063d887aec205e0d3e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />) is not bounded above, meaning you can eventually surpass any real number by adding enough whole numbers together.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Statement\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Statement</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#For_Real_Numbers\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">For Real Numbers</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Equivalent_Formulation\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Equivalent Formulation</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Proof_(Using_the_Supremum_Property)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof (Using the Supremum Property)</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Proof_by_Contradiction\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof by Contradiction</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Applications\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Applications</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Statement\">Statement</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Archimedean_property&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Statement\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"For_Real_Numbers\">For Real Numbers</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Archimedean_property&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: For Real Numbers\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>In the context of the real numbers (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/6/906fddead65545297c74bbc2ca83cd01f3f9ecc9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}$\" width=\"12\" height=\"12\" />), the Archimedean property can be formally stated as follows:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/9/499206beca89b1c17841a3af2722c67452df23bb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\forall x, y \\in \\mathbb{R} \\text{ with } x &gt; 0, \\exists n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\text{ such that } nx &gt; y\\]\" width=\"358\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>In plain terms, if you take any positive number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> (no matter how small) and add it to itself enough times (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> times), it will eventually grow larger than any other number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> (no matter how big).\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Equivalent_Formulation\">Equivalent Formulation</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Archimedean_property&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Equivalent Formulation\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Another common way to express this property in <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Calculus\" title=\"Calculus\">calculus</a> and mathematical analysis is:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/4/1e4690f02452ea0cd5497a128132252be3a264f4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\forall \\varepsilon &gt; 0, \\exists n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\text{ such that } \\frac{1}{n} &lt; \\varepsilon\\]\" width=\"242\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>This ensures that the sequence of fractions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/9/f49799634fa29d2f06102ee1b7698ff737302a84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{1}, \\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{3}, \\dots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"90\" height=\"38\" /> approaches zero, guaranteeing that there are no positive real numbers that are \"infinitely close\" to zero without being zero.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Proof_.28Using_the_Supremum_Property.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof_(Using_the_Supremum_Property)\">Proof (Using the Supremum Property)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Archimedean_property&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Proof (Using the Supremum Property)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>While treated as an axiom in abstract algebraic structures, the Archimedean property can be proven as a theorem in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/6/906fddead65545297c74bbc2ca83cd01f3f9ecc9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}$\" width=\"12\" height=\"12\" /> using the <b>Completeness Axiom</b> (the Least Upper Bound Property) of real numbers.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof_by_Contradiction\">Proof by Contradiction</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Archimedean_property&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Proof by Contradiction\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Assume for the sake of contradiction that the Archimedean property does not hold. \n</p>\n<ol><li><b>Negation of the Statement:</b> If the property is false, then there exist some positive real numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> such that for every positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />:</li></ol>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/b/7db801934c8df96e5b5de2f7dfb4cfaba13d284b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[nx \\le y\\]\" width=\"54\" height=\"15\" />\n</p>\n<ol><li><b>Defining a Bounded Set:</b> Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> be the set of all multiples of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> by a natural number:</li></ol>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/7/f476fbfe9de5a47504b0de10a87b011aabca73a1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[S = \\{nx : n \\in \\mathbb{N}\\}\\]\" width=\"135\" height=\"18\" />\nBecause <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/1/2612b8735068278fbe85e36d623bcde7512feae7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$nx \\le y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"57\" height=\"17\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is bounded above by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p>\n<ol><li><b>Applying Completeness:</b> By the Least Upper Bound Axiom, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> is non-empty and bounded above, it must have a supremum (a least upper bound) in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/6/906fddead65545297c74bbc2ca83cd01f3f9ecc9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}$\" width=\"12\" height=\"12\" />. Let this least upper bound be called <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/f/10f32377ac67d94f764f12a15ea987e88c85d3e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\alpha$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" />:</li></ol>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/3/92321f7cbcf0e2a2216383eb6ca74deaedced14c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\alpha = \\sup(S)\\]\" width=\"87\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<ol><li><b>Generating the Contradiction:</b> Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/f/10f32377ac67d94f764f12a15ea987e88c85d3e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\alpha$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> is the <i>least</i> upper bound, any number smaller than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/f/10f32377ac67d94f764f12a15ea987e88c85d3e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\alpha$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> cannot be an upper bound. Because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/e/cae4fd68f44ac9b909f8d91f9d498de5759bb45b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x &gt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" />, the number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/8/328fee9bb529156794127ca3e3cf8f0864b80af2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\alpha - x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> is strictly less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/f/10f32377ac67d94f764f12a15ea987e88c85d3e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\alpha$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" />.</li>\n<li>Therefore, there must exist some element in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> that is strictly greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/8/328fee9bb529156794127ca3e3cf8f0864b80af2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\alpha - x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />:</li></ol>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/0/8e0335391d9024fc04318b579e16849a74842c79.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[m x &gt; \\alpha - x \\quad \\text{for some } m \\in \\mathbb{N}\\]\" width=\"229\" height=\"13\" />\n</p>\n<ol><li>Adding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> to both sides of the inequality yields:</li></ol>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2b106b94b1437a34bb2a081494a9c02ffd4fd09.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(m + 1)x &gt; \\alpha\\]\" width=\"105\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<ol><li>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/1/f21b49d4a1d07db0896300cd7c894fdbb50883a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m \\in \\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"13\" />, it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/919e479b276bcd240b3afe35c4e5b9a4869ef7a7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(m + 1) \\in \\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"98\" height=\"20\" />, which means <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/1/9e1c7dfc57c4e7a375c719fecabc31c15222113d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(m + 1)x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"74\" height=\"20\" /> is an element of our set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />.</li>\n<li>We have found an element in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" /> that is strictly greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/f/10f32377ac67d94f764f12a15ea987e88c85d3e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\alpha$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" />. This contradicts our initial definition that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/f/10f32377ac67d94f764f12a15ea987e88c85d3e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\alpha$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> is an upper bound for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/2/ad28c83c99a8fd0dd2e2e594c9d02ee532765a0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />.</li></ol>\n<p>Because our assumption leads directly to a logical contradiction, the assumption must be false. Therefore, the Archimedean property holds true for all real numbers. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Applications\">Applications</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Archimedean_property&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Applications\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Infinitude_of_integers\" title=\"Infinitude of integers\">Infinitude of integers</a>:</b> Setting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/3/34310f2f36ed6d724838edb08788ee62acb33386.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" /> in the property directly proves that the natural numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/e/a1e6cd0e4103015efba12e0fc4291f012b467eaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> (and therefore the integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/0/d4023589a374e5b4a1bc6063d887aec205e0d3e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"14\" />) are not bounded above by any finite boundary.</li>\n<li><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Limit\" title=\"Limit\">Limits</a> in Calculus:</b> It forms the foundational logical backbone required to prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/4/ec4a5ab94fd73c204f1c7b64ee1567ed09a85925.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{n} = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"88\" height=\"38\" />, which is the starting point for almost all rigorous limits.</li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Archimedean_property&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Proof_by_contradiction\" title=\"Proof by contradiction\">Proof by contradiction</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Calculus\" title=\"Calculus\">Calculus</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Set_theory\" title=\"Set theory\">Set theory</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Archimedean_property&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017190231\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.032 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.059 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 266/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 301/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 8399/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.798      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.798      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:37613:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017190231 and revision id 291244. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}